      "narrative": "Pima County’s residential market is in a mildly cooling but still active phase: June’s median sale price of $385,000 was down about 1.3% year over year, framed locally as a small pause within a longer upward price trend rather than a true reversal.[1] Inventory has risen to about 3.1 months of supply with 3,329 active listings, and roughly half of all listings have taken price cuts, signaling more negotiation room and a shift away from the extreme seller leverage of past years.[1] Despite this, buyer activity remains solid, closed sales in June were up 4.5% year over year to 1,065 homes, around 81% of listings are going pending or closing, and homes still go under contract in a median of 33 days, suggesting a balanced-to-slightly buyer-leaning market rather than a slump.[1] Local dynamics are shaped by steady in-migration into the Tucson/Pima area and ongoing, though moderating, construction statewide,[12][15] with affordability relative to Phoenix and continued employment growth in Tucson’s education, defense, and healthcare sectors providing underlying support to demand.[19][11]",
